================================================================================
HOW TO BUILD AND RUN
================================================================================

REQUIREMENTS
------------
    - GCC compiler (tested on iLab with gcc)
    - GNU Make
    - All source files in the same directory

BUILDING
--------
To compile, run:

    make

This produces the following executables:

    chatd        — the chat server
    test_client  — the automated test harness

To remove all compiled files and start fresh:

    make clean


RUNNING THE SERVER
------------------
    ./chatd <port>

Example:

    ./chatd 19234

The server will listen indefinitely on the given port. Use a port above
1024 to avoid permission issues. On shared machines like iLab, pick a
high port (e.g. 19234) to avoid collisions with other users.


CONNECTING CLIENTS
------------------
Using the provided sample client:

    ./clients/client <name> <host> <port>

Example (open two terminals):

    ./clients/client Alice 127.0.0.1 19234
    ./clients/client Bob   127.0.0.1 19234

Client commands:
    <message>          send a message to everyone
    /<status>          set your status
    ?                  query all users and their statuses
    ?<name>            query a specific user
    @<name> <message>  send a private message


RUNNING THE TEST SUITE
----------------------
    ./chatd 19234 &
    ./test_client 127.0.0.1 19234

Runs 15 automated protocol-level tests and prints PASS/FAIL for each,
with a final score at the bottom.

================================================================================
DESIGN NOTES
================================================================================

OVERVIEW
--------
chatd is a multithreaded TCP chat server. The main thread accepts incoming
connections in a loop; each accepted connection is handed off to a newly
spawned, detached client thread. A global clients[] array protected by a
single mutex tracks every connected, named user.

FILES + PURPOSES
----------------

================================================================================
FILE: chatd.c
================================================================================

Purpose: entry point. Parses the port argument, creates the listening socket
with SO_REUSEADDR, and runs the accept loop. For each incoming connection:

    1. Finds a free slot in the clients[] table under the mutex.
    2. Stores the new fd and clears the name/status fields.
    3. Allocates a slot index on the heap and passes it to a new detached
       pthread, then releases it.

================================================================================
FILE: client.c
================================================================================

Purpose: implements client_thread() and all message handlers. Each thread
owns one connection for its lifetime. Structured in the following stages:

    1. valid_name:       checks that a screen name contains only letters,
                         digits, hyphens, and underscores.

    2. valid_printable:  checks that a string contains only characters in
                         the range 32-126 (printable ASCII).

    3. broadcast_locked: sends a MSG to every named client. Must be called
                         with clients_mutex held.

    4. client_thread:    main per-client loop. Calls read_message() in a
                         loop and dispatches on the message type:

                         NAM — validates the name, checks for duplicates
                               under the mutex, registers the user, sends
                               the welcome message.

                         SET — validates and stores the status. If non-empty,
                               broadcasts the status change to #all.

                         MSG — parses sender/recip/body fields. Validates
                               body length and characters. Broadcasts to
                               #all or unicasts to the named recipient.

                         WHO — builds a response string using open_memstream.
                               For #all, lists every named user with status.
                               For a specific name, reports their status or
                               "No status".

    5. disconnect:       checks clients[slot].fd == fd before clearing the
                         slot (guards against a slow thread racing with a
                         newly accepted client that reused the slot). Closes
                         the fd and broadcasts the departure message.

================================================================================
FILE: proto.c
================================================================================

Purpose: protocol I/O. Implements the wire format 1|<TYPE>|<BODYLEN>|<BODY|.

    1. read_exact:    reads exactly n bytes from a fd, retrying on short reads.

    2. read_message:  reads one complete protocol message. Parses the version,
                      type, and body-length fields byte-by-byte (reading up to
                      each '|'), then bulk-reads exactly BODYLEN bytes. Sends
                      ERR 0 and returns 0 on any protocol violation.

    3. send_msg:      formats and writes a 1|MSG|...|  frame.

    4. send_err:      formats and writes a 1|ERR|...|  frame.

================================================================================
FILES: proto.h, client.h
================================================================================

    proto.h  — protocol constants (MAX_NAME, MAX_STATUS, MAX_MSG) and
               declarations for read_message, send_msg, send_err.

    client.h — Client struct definition, MAX_CLIENTS, extern declarations
               for the global clients[] table and clients_mutex, and the
               client_thread prototype.

================================================================================
ALGORITHM: client_thread main loop
================================================================================

    Step 1: Save fd and slot index. Set named = 0.

    Step 2: Call read_message(). On EOF or IO error, go to disconnect.

    Step 3: If type is NAM:
        - Validate name characters and length.
        - Acquire mutex, check for duplicates, register name, release mutex.
        - Send welcome MSG. Set named = 1. Loop.

    Step 4: If not yet named and type is not NAM, send ERR 0 and disconnect.

    Step 5: If type is SET:
        - Validate status characters and length.
        - Acquire mutex, store status, broadcast if non-empty, release mutex.

    Step 6: If type is MSG:
        - Parse body into sender (ignored), recip, message fields.
        - Validate message characters and length.
        - Acquire mutex. Broadcast to #all or unicast to named recipient.
        - Release mutex.

    Step 7: If type is WHO:
        - Acquire mutex. Build response with open_memstream.
        - Send MSG back to the querying client. Release mutex.

    Step 8: On unknown type, send ERR 0 and disconnect.

    Step 9 (disconnect): Acquire mutex. If clients[slot].fd == fd, clear the
        slot and broadcast departure. Release mutex. Close fd.

================================================================================
TEST PLAN
================================================================================

Testing was performed with test_client.c, an automated harness that opens
fresh TCP connections for each test case and uses a 3-second per-read
timeout to catch hangs. All 34 assertions pass against chatd on iLab.

Additional manual testing was done using the provided sample client binary
with three simultaneous users (Alice, Bob, Carol) to verify broadcasts,
private messages, status updates, WHO queries, and departure announcements.

T1  NAM welcome
    A new client sends a valid NAM. Server replies with MSG containing
    "Welcome".

T2  Duplicate name (ERR 1)
    Two clients attempt to use the same screen name. The second receives
    ERR with code 1.

T3  Illegal character in name (ERR 3)
    NAM with a '!' in the name is rejected with ERR code 3.

T4  Name too long (ERR 4)
    NAM with a 33-character name is rejected with ERR code 4.

T5  SET broadcasts to room
    Client sets a non-empty status. Both the setter and an observer
    receive a MSG from #all announcing the new status.

T6  MSG to #all forwarded
    A message sent to #all is forwarded to all named clients with the
    correct sender field filled in by the server.

T7  Private MSG
    A message sent to a specific user is delivered only to that user
    with the correct sender field. The third user does not receive it.

T8  MSG to unknown recipient (ERR 2)
    Sending to a name not currently in the chat returns ERR code 2.

T9  WHO <user> with status
    Querying a user who has set a status returns "name: status".

T10 WHO #all
    Querying #all returns a newline-separated list of all named clients
    with their statuses.

T11 Bad protocol version (ERR 0)
    A message with version "9" is rejected with ERR code 0 (fatal).

T12 WHO unknown user (ERR 2)
    Querying a name not in the chat returns ERR code 2.

T13 SET empty status suppresses broadcast
    SET with an empty body does not broadcast. Subsequent WHO reports
    "No status".

T14 MSG body too long (ERR 4)
    An 81-character message body (over the 80-char limit) is rejected
    with ERR code 4.

T15 SET status too long (ERR 4)
    A 65-character status (over the 64-char limit) is rejected with
    ERR code 4.

================================================================================
ERROR HANDLING
================================================================================

    1. ERR 0 (unreadable) is fatal — the server closes the connection after
       sending it. ERR 1-4 are recoverable; the server waits for the client
       to send a corrected message.

    2. Unknown message types send ERR 0 and close the connection.

    3. Malformed MSG bodies (missing field separators) send ERR 0 and close
       the connection.

    4. If the server is full (128 clients), the new connection receives ERR 0
       and is closed immediately.

    5. If a client sends any message type other than NAM before completing
       registration, the server sends ERR 0 and closes the connection.

================================================================================
KNOWN LIMITATIONS + ASSUMPTIONS
================================================================================

    1. A maximum of 128 simultaneous clients is supported (MAX_CLIENTS).

    2. The server does not support multiple rooms — all users are in #all.

    3. WHO #all lists users in no particular order, as the spec permits.

    4. In case a recipient name has illegal characters, we don't even bother to
       commit to the operation of getting lock, checking our array of clients, and determining
       no client existing. We simply return that client name has an illegal character. Given the 
       wording of the project description, error code 2 (inactive screen name) or 
       3 (invalid character) both seem justifiable, but we stuck with error code 3.
    
    5. If we receive an unknown message type, we default to error code 0.
